# Input graph for
# INCATools__kgcl-rdflib__kgcl_rdflib_diff_owlstar_sublanguage.py__get_bnodes_2_triple_annotations.
#
# get_bnodes_2_triple_annotations(g) reads two different shapes:
#   * a full-collection read (set(g.subjects(predicate=P))) for each of the
#     three OWL reification predicates, intersected -> the bnodes that
#     carry all three;
#   * for each such bnode, the trav_single_value site itself: THREE
#     next(g.objects(subject=i, predicate=P)) singleton reads, plus a
#     subject-rooted g.triples((i, None, None)) scan of everything else.
#
@prefix ex:  <http://example.org/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .

# SEVERAL solutions -- two complete reified-triple annotations, each with
# its own extra triples: ex:comment/ex:weight (plain objects, must be
# collected) and, on _:ann1, ex:extra pointing at a BLANK NODE object
# (must be EXCLUDED by the `not isinstance(o, BNode)` filter).
_:ann1 owl:annotatedSource   ex:Alice ;
       owl:annotatedProperty ex:knows ;
       owl:annotatedTarget   ex:Bob ;
       ex:comment "friendly" ;
       ex:extra [ ex:nested "must be excluded: BNode object" ] .

_:ann2 owl:annotatedSource   ex:Carol ;
       owl:annotatedProperty ex:knows ;
       owl:annotatedTarget   ex:Dave ;
       ex:weight 5 .

# Structural ZERO-solution case: missing owl:annotatedTarget, so this bnode
# is absent from source & property & target -- it must not appear in
# `annotations` at all (not even as an empty list). This is the fixture's
# zero-solution branch: `next()`/`m{ }.first()` divergence on an EMPTY
# per-bnode read is otherwise unreachable here, since the intersection
# construction guarantees every `i` the inner reads run against already
# has all three predicates (see meta.json).
_:partial owl:annotatedSource   ex:Erin ;
          owl:annotatedProperty ex:knows .

# Edge case: a NAMED (non-blank) subject satisfying all three predicates.
# It DOES enter the intersection (the read is keyed on the predicates, not
# on node kind) but `isinstance(i, BNode)` then skips it entirely -- it
# must appear in `annotations` with an EMPTY list, and ex:comment below
# must never be read.
ex:NamedAnnotation owl:annotatedSource   ex:Frank ;
                    owl:annotatedProperty ex:knows ;
                    owl:annotatedTarget   ex:Grace ;
                    ex:comment "must never be read" .

# NEIGHBOURHOOD that must not match -- only ONE of the three required
# predicates: matches the individual `source` read but must drop out of
# the source & property & target intersection.
ex:Decoy owl:annotatedSource ex:Henry .

# NEIGHBOURHOOD, asymmetric the other way -- annotatedProperty AND
# annotatedTarget, but NOT annotatedSource. Distinguishes the `source`
# read from the `property` read: a translation that collected `source`
# from the wrong predicate (e.g. annotatedProperty) would wrongly pull
# this decoy into the intersection too (verified: swapping `source`'s
# predicate for owl:annotatedProperty was NOT caught before this decoy
# was added, and IS caught now -- see meta.json).
ex:Decoy2 owl:annotatedProperty ex:knows ;
          owl:annotatedTarget   ex:Ivy .
